# Bug hunt trigger

When the user asks to run a bug hunt in any chat on this repo — the message is or clearly requests a bug hunt, including typos and derivatives such as "bug hunt", "bu hunt", "bug hun", "bung hunt", "do a bug hunt", "do a bung hunt", "bug-hunt", "bughunt", "/bug-hunt", "hunt for bugs", "hunt bugs", "hunting bugs", "run a bug hunt", "start a bug hunt", "perform a bug hunt", "bug hunting", "bug hunts", "hunt some bugs", "hunting for more bugs", "run another loop to fix bugs", "loop to fix bugs" — read `.cursor/commands/bug-hunt.md` and do one iteration of that script in this turn, then end the turn. If that file is missing, read `scripts/bug-hunt.md`. One iteration is one inference pass. After the outcome row is written, write the continue file named in that script and end the turn. The stop hook waits the script's pause: a positive number of seconds is not skippable, and 0 means it does not wait. Then the hook starts the next inference pass. Do not ask the user to continue. Do not stop after one bug by starting the next bug in this same turn. End the turn after the iteration. The hook starts the next pass unless a stop condition in the script is met. Do not reply with instructions for how to invoke the command.

Match intent, not only an exact string: "please hunt for bugs", "start hunting bugs", "run a bug-hunt", or "/bug-hunt" are start signals. A question about the command itself (whether it works, where the file is, how to install it) is not a start signal. A request to stop, cancel, pause, skip, disable, or wait for the hunt is not a start signal. A request to review, summarize, audit, or explain the bug hunt script is not a start signal. "end to end bug hunt" and a start after another clause ("stop explaining and run a bug hunt") are start signals.
